      "narration": "Now the failure that defines the whole field. Usopp decides to become a great sniper, so he practises for a month in Syrup Village. Same range every day, same three trees, the same steady afternoon wind off the same hill, the same distance paced out from the same fence post. By the end of the month he is flawless — on that range. Then the crew reaches the Grand Line, where the wind reverses direction halfway through the flight of the shot, and he misses everything. Usopp did not learn to shoot. He memorized one island. That is overfitting, and once you have that picture you can diagnose it forever. Concretely, overfitting is a shape you see in two curves. Training loss keeps falling, smoothly, encouragingly. Validation loss falls for a while, reaches a minimum, and then starts climbing while the training loss is still improving. That crossing point is the moment the model stopped learning the signal and started fitting the noise — the accidents of this particular sample, the specific gust on that specific afternoon, which will never repeat. The opposite failure is underfitting: both curves stay high and flat, because the hypothesis space you allowed was too small to contain the truth. Fitting a straight line to a circle, no matter how long you train, leaves you with a straight line. This is why the data split is non-negotiable, and why it has three parts, not two. The training set fits the parameters. The validation set chooses the settings — how deep, how much regularization, when to stop. The test set is measured once, at the very end, and never again. The moment you choose anything by looking at the test score, the test set has quietly become a training set with extra steps, and your reported number is a fiction. And finally, learn to read the gap rather than the level. A model at ninety-nine percent on training and seventy-one on validation is worse than a model at eighty-four and eighty-two. The second one actually learned the task. The first one memorized Syrup Village.",
