      "heading": "Regularization: making training deliberately harder",
      "narration": "So overfitting is the enemy. The counter-attack is called regularization, and the idea behind every variant is the same: deliberately make training harder, so the model cannot afford to memorize. The classic form adds a penalty to the loss. In L2 regularization, also called ridge, you add lambda times the sum of the squared weights. Think about what this changes. Before, a huge weight was free; if it bought even a sliver of training accuracy, the optimizer took it. Now every large weight costs something, so the model will only grow one when the evidence genuinely supports it. The result is a gentler fit that shrinks all the weights toward zero without any of them quite arriving. L1, also called lasso, adds lambda times the sum of the absolute values instead. This looks like a small change and is not. The absolute value has a corner at zero, and that corner does something the smooth square cannot: it drives weights exactly to zero and leaves them there. So L1 performs feature selection as a side effect. It does not just hand you a gentler model, it hands you a shorter one, with the useless inputs switched off entirely — which is worth a great deal when you have to explain the model to somebody. In both cases lambda is the dial, and it is chosen on the validation set, never on training. Lambda of zero is no regularization at all. Lambda approaching infinity crushes every weight to zero, and your model predicts the same mean value for everything. The useful setting is somewhere between, and the only honest way to find it is to sweep a range and look at where the validation curve bottoms out. And finally, do not let the word 'regularization' shrink to mean 'penalty term'. Early stopping is regularization: you halt while the model is still too simple to memorize. Dropout is regularization. Augmenting your data with rotations and crops is regularization. And plain more data is the strongest regularizer of all. They all do one job — restrict what the model is able to express, so that the only thing left worth expressing is the signal.",
