      "heading": "Cross-validation and the discipline that protects you",
      "narration": "One last piece, and it is the one that separates people whose numbers hold up from people whose numbers collapse in production. A single train-validation split is itself a random draw. If your dataset is small, the model you crown as best may simply be the one that got lucky about which rows landed in which half. The fix is k-fold cross-validation. Split the data into k parts — five and ten are the usual choices. Train k times, each time holding out a different part as the validation set, and average the k scores you get. Now your estimate rests on all the data instead of one arbitrary slice. But the average is only half the value, and most people stop there. Look at the spread of those k scores. A mean of nought-point-eight-six with a spread of plus or minus nought-point-nought-one is a result you can act on. The same mean with a spread of plus or minus nought-point-nought-nine means you have measured almost nothing, and if you rank two models whose difference is smaller than that spread, you are ranking noise. This is the same lesson as the confidence interval island, arriving in a new costume. Now two traps that ruin real projects. First: never shuffle time. If your data is ordered in time — prices, weather, logs, anything sequential — random folds let the model train on Thursday and validate on Wednesday, which is peeking at the future. Train on the past, validate on the future, always. The same applies to grouped data: all the rows belonging to one patient, one user, one ship must sit in the same fold, or the model recognizes the individual rather than learning the pattern. And second, the rule that survives every framework and every fashion: fit every transformation on the training fold only. Scaling, imputation of missing values, feature selection, vocabulary building — all of it learns from the training fold, and is then applied to the held-out fold. Fitting a scaler on the whole dataset before splitting feels harmless and is the most common silent leak in this entire field. It will not throw an error. It will simply give you a number that is too good, and you will not find out until production.",
